            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>The Verdict First</h2>
<p>In Meta advertising, "high-performing" creatives and "revenue-generating" creatives are fundamentally different things. High-performing creatives get impressions and clicks; revenue-generating creatives deliver acceptable CPA and ROAS. <strong>A proper ad review must separate these two dimensions</strong>, not evaluate them together based on gut feeling.</p>
<p>Many newer advertisers and even experienced media buyers make one common mistake: seeing a 3% CTR and $8 CPM leads them to conclude the creative is "performing well." But if that creative's CPA is $50 while the product's average order value is only $40, the creative has already failed from a business perspective—it gets views but doesn't sell.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Judging Whether a Creative "Performs": Key Metrics</h2>
<p>"High-performing" means the creative gets sufficient impressions and engagement within Meta's algorithm. Focus on these indicators:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>CTR (Click-Through Rate)</strong>: Industry benchmarks vary by category. For utility apps, 2%-4% is acceptable; for e-commerce, 1%-2% is normal. Below 0.5% warrants attention.</li>
<li><strong>CPM (Cost Per Mille)</strong>: Lower CPM indicates stronger appeal to the target audience and better demographic match.</li>
<li><strong>Video Retention</strong>: Particularly important on TikTok and Instagram Reels. First-3-second retention and overall completion rates directly determine whether the algorithm continues distributing the content.</li>
<li><strong>Engagement Rate</strong>: Likes, comments, shares—these signals tell the platform that users "want to interact with this content."</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Practical Experience</strong>: A cross-border e-commerce team testing home goods creatives found one with a $6 CPM and 2.8% CTR, but a $35 CPA. Post-analysis revealed the creative attracted middle-aged female users who "looked but didn't buy," severely mismatched with the product's target demographic. This creative performed but didn't sell.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Judging Whether a Creative "Sells": Commercial Metrics</h2>
<p>"Revenue-generating" is the ultimate goal of advertising. To determine if a creative brings real money, focus on:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>CPA (Cost Per Acquisition)</strong>: Average cost to acquire a user who completes registration or purchase. If it exceeds LTV (Lifetime Value) by more than 30%, immediate optimization or pausing is needed.</li>
<li><strong>ROAS (Return on Ad Spend)</strong>: Requirements vary by category. E-commerce typically requires ROAS ≥3; utility apps depend on paying user rates and payment depth.</li>
<li><strong>CVR (Conversion Rate)</strong>: Landing page or in-app conversion. E-commerce landing pages with CVR below 1% face either traffic targeting or landing page experience issues.</li>
<li><strong>First-Order Recovery Period</strong>: Especially critical for DTC brands. Whether ad costs recover within 7 or 14 days directly determines whether scaling is viable.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>A Simple Formula</strong>:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Revenue-generating creatives = High-performing creatives + Precise targeting + Well-structured landing page</p>
</blockquote>
<p>All three are essential. No matter how well a creative performs, if clicked users aren't your target customers, commercial value is zero.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Two Questions to Always Ask During Reviews</h2>
<p>In every creative review meeting, have your team answer these two questions before diving into details:</p>
<ol>
<li>
<p><strong>Is this creative's traffic precise?</strong><br />
   Not all traffic is effective traffic. Broad traffic boosts CTR but lowers CVR and ROAS.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Will users acquired through this creative generate sustained value?</strong><br />
   Some creatives drive first orders but have极低 repeat purchase rates. This "selling" is also short-term.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<hr />
<h2>The Right Way to Review</h2>
<p>Don't draw conclusions based on single metrics alone. The recommended review logic:</p>
<ol>
<li>Check ROAS and CPA first to filter out commercially unviable creatives</li>
<li>Examine CTR and CPM to identify "high-performing" creatives</li>
<li>Compare the two data sets to find creatives that perform AND sell</li>
<li>Focus analysis on "high-performing but non-revenue" creatives—is the issue audience targeting or landing page?</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Bottom Line</strong>: High performance is the foundation; revenue generation is the purpose. Teams that only optimize CTR without optimizing ROAS are always wasting money.</p>
